const body = `<h1>The String Class</h1>

<p>
    You've been using Strings since hello world. But String is not a primitive — it's
    an <strong>object</strong>. That means it comes with methods. A LOT of them.
</p>

<h2>Strings Are Objects</h2>

<pre><code>String name = "Frodo";          // shortcut — Java does this for you
String name2 = new String("Frodo"); // explicit — same thing

// Strings have methods. Call them with a dot.
System.out.println(name.length());  // 5 — counts characters
System.out.println(name.toUpperCase()); // "FRODO"</code></pre>

<p>
    <strong>Important:</strong> Strings are <em>immutable</em>. Methods like
    <code>toUpperCase()</code> don't change the original — they return a new String.
</p>

<pre><code>String s = "hello";
s.toUpperCase();
System.out.println(s); // still "hello" — the original didn't change!

s = s.toUpperCase();   // NOW s is "HELLO" — we reassigned</code></pre>

<h2>The Essential Methods</h2>

<h3>length() — how many characters</h3>
<pre><code>"dragon".length();   // 6
"".length();          // 0 — empty string
" ".length();         // 1 — space is a character</code></pre>

<h3>substring(start, end) — slice out part</h3>
<pre><code>String s = "dungeon";
s.substring(0, 3);    // "dun" — characters 0,1,2 (stops BEFORE 3)
s.substring(3);       // "geon" — from 3 to end</code></pre>

<h3>indexOf(thing) — find position</h3>
<pre><code>"sword".indexOf("o");   // 2 — first 'o' is at index 2
"sword".indexOf("z");   // -1 — not found
"sword".indexOf("or");  // 2 — finds whole substring</code></pre>

<h3>equals(other) — compare contents</h3>
<pre><code>String a = "sword";
String b = "sword";
String c = "shield";

a.equals(b);  // true — same characters
a.equals(c);  // false

// NEVER use == to compare Strings!
a == b        // SOMETIMES true, sometimes false — DON'T DO THIS</code></pre>

<h3>compareTo(other) — ordering</h3>
<pre><code>"axe".compareTo("bow");     // negative — "axe" comes before "bow"
"bow".compareTo("axe");     // positive
"axe".compareTo("axe");     // 0 — they're equal</code></pre>

<h2>Tinker Challenge: String Slicing</h2>

<div class="callout lab">
<pre><code>String weapon = "Excalibur";
System.out.println(weapon.length());
System.out.println(weapon.substring(0, 3));
System.out.println(weapon.indexOf("cal"));
System.out.println(weapon.substring(weapon.indexOf("cal")));</code></pre>
<p>Write your prediction for each line. Then run and check. Did the second substring behave differently than the first? Why?</p>
</div>

<h2>String + Concatenation</h2>

<pre><code>int hp = 42;
System.out.println("HP: " + hp);          // "HP: 42"
System.out.println("Damage: " + (10 + 5)); // "Damage: 15"
System.out.println("Damage: " + 10 + 5);   // "Damage: 105" — TRAP!</code></pre>

<p>+ runs left-to-right. <code>"Damage: " + 10</code> becomes <code>"Damage: 10"</code>,
then <code>"Damage: 10" + 5</code> becomes <code>"Damage: 105"</code>.
Use parentheses: <code>"Damage: " + (10 + 5)</code>.</p>

<h2>Bug Hunt: String Traps</h2>

<div class="callout lab">
    <strong>Bug 1: The Identical Twins</strong><br>
    <pre><code>String s1 = new String("magic");
String s2 = new String("magic");
if (s1 == s2) {
    System.out.println("They match!");
} else {
    System.out.println("They are different!");
}</code></pre>
    <p>Run this. Why does it say they are different? Fix the code so it correctly identifies them as matching.</p>
</div>

<div class="callout lab">
    <strong>Bug 2: The Stubborn String</strong><br>
    <pre><code>String word = "dragon";
word.toUpperCase();
System.out.println(word);</code></pre>
    <p>Run this. Why is it still lowercase? Fix the code so it prints "DRAGON".</p>
</div>

<h2>Methods the Exam Sheet Does Not Have</h2>

<p>
    Java's String class has more methods than the exam gives you. The
    <a href="../../exam/java-quick-reference.html">Java Quick Reference</a> lists only
    <code>length</code>, <code>substring</code> (both forms),
    <code>indexOf(String)</code>, <code>equals</code>, <code>compareTo</code>, and
    <code>split</code>. Everything below is standard Java, and some of it shows up in
    this unit's labs. Learn the substitute next to it, because on exam day the
    substitute is what you get.
</p>

<h3>contains(text): use indexOf and compare to -1</h3>
<pre><code>String password = "pass@word";

password.contains("@");         // true. Works in Java, not on the exam sheet
password.indexOf("@") != -1;    // true. This is the version to write
password.indexOf("#") != -1;    // false. indexOf answers -1 when nothing matches</code></pre>
<p>
    <code>!= -1</code> is doing the work. <code>indexOf</code> answers "where is it," and
    <code>-1</code> is its way of saying "nowhere." So "does this String contain that"
    becomes "is the position anything other than -1." Watch the trap: <code>0</code>
    means found at the very start, which is a hit, not a miss.
</p>

<h3>charAt(i): pull one character out with substring</h3>
<pre><code>String weapon = "Excalibur";

weapon.charAt(0);                     // 'E'. A char, and charAt is not on the sheet
weapon.substring(0, 1);               // "E". A one-character String, and this IS on the sheet
weapon.substring(0, 1).equals("E");   // true. Compare it like any other String</code></pre>
<p>
    The sheet's String list has no <code>charAt</code>, so the one-character
    <code>substring</code> is the version to write. It is also the friendlier one: you get
    a String back, and you already know how to compare Strings. Unit 4 walks whole
    Strings this way, one character at a time.
</p>

<h3>trim(): removes the spaces Scanner leaves behind</h3>
<pre><code>String raw = "  attack goblin  ";
raw.trim();   // "attack goblin"

System.out.println(raw);   // "  attack goblin  ". raw itself never changed</code></pre>
<p>
    <code>trim()</code> strips leading and trailing whitespace, and it does not modify the
    original (immutability again). It is not on the sheet either, but this course uses it
    whenever data arrives from a file or the keyboard, because stray spaces are normal
    there. Without it, you find the edges yourself: the first character that is not a
    space and the last one that is.
</p>

<h3>Finding the second occurrence</h3>
<p>
    The sheet gives you the one-argument <code>indexOf(String)</code>, so it reports the
    first match and stops. To find the next one, search what is left over:
</p>
<pre><code>String commands = "attack goblin attack dragon";

int first  = commands.indexOf("attack");                            // 0
int rest   = first + 1;                                             // skip past the match
int second = commands.substring(rest).indexOf("attack") + rest;     // 14</code></pre>
<p>
    Read it from the inside out: cut off everything through the first match, search that
    shorter String, then add back how much you cut. Java also has
    <code>indexOf(text, fromIndex)</code>, a two-argument form that does this in a single
    call. It is convenient in your own programs and it is not on the sheet, so the
    composition above is the one worth knowing.
</p>

<h3>Comparing without caring about case</h3>
<pre><code>String typed = "PassWord";

typed.toLowerCase().indexOf("password") != -1;   // true
typed.toLowerCase().equals("password");          // true</code></pre>
<p>
    <code>toLowerCase()</code> and <code>toUpperCase()</code> are everywhere in this
    unit's labs and neither is on the sheet. Use them when you build. When a task needs a
    case-insensitive comparison, chain them onto the methods you already have, which is
    exactly what the password validator asks you to do.
</p>

<h3>StringBuilder: building a long string without the copies</h3>
<pre><code>String log = "";
log += "attack";    // a brand-new String every time
log += " goblin";   // "attack goblin"

StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();   // not on the sheet
sb.append("attack");
sb.append(" goblin");
sb.toString();      // "attack goblin"</code></pre>
<p>
    Every <code>+=</code> builds a whole new String, because a String cannot be
    changed in place. <code>StringBuilder</code> appends to one growing buffer
    instead, and <code>toString()</code> hands back the finished String at the
    end. It is the faster tool for assembling a String in a loop, and it is not on
    the sheet: <code>+=</code> is the version you write on exam day, and at the
    sizes you will handle there, <code>+=</code> is fine.
</p>

<h2>Want More?</h2>
